.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt BSDFILESYSTEM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDFilesystem
.Nd "[TZFS] storage daemon"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the
.Sy [TZFS]
system daemon that owns the storage plane of the capability system.
It provisions persistent and ephemeral dataset roots beneath a selected
base dataset capability.
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.Filesystem
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel with the FIDL library
.Li bsd.filesystem.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/filesystemcmp.fidl .
A session calls
.Sy GetProtocolInfo
first; a method before it, an unknown method, a malformed frame, a pre-FIDL
record or an attached descriptor closes the session without a reply.
Application errors are
.Xr errno 2
values.
The discovery
.Em domain
layer resolves
.Dq Li system.Filesystem
only for authorized clients.
.Pp
Obtaining storage is
.Em consumer self-service :
a program asks for a dataset, configuration object, or isolated scratch root at
runtime by calling
.Xr service_storage_open 3 ,
.Xr service_open_config 3 ,
or
.Xr service_open_isolated 3 ,
whose library resolves
.Nm
and drives it.
On request
.Nm
mints a rights-limited TrustedZFS handle
.Pq see Xr trustedzfs 3
scoped to the caller's unforgeable channel label and re-delivers it over that
channel; the consumer then mounts and drives the handle itself.
A claim may ask for the store
.Em mounted
.Pq the sandboxed consumer cannot mount it itself
or for a mounted
.Em read-only view ,
whose directory carries read-only Capsicum rights so every descriptor derived
under it can look up, read, stat and map but never write, create, unlink or
change attributes; this is how a bundle's units read the shared environment
one of them writes.
A store is mounted once and shared by every holder; it stays mounted while
any holder keeps the delivered directory descriptor
.Pq so a restart of Nm never unmounts a store under a running consumer
and is unmounted when the last such descriptor and the last claim are gone;
.Nm
keeps one mount anchor per claim, so a consumer may hold its persistent
store, its cache and shared stores at the same time over its one connection.
.Nm
is never driven by
.Xr switchboard 8
on a unit's behalf, and a unit does not declare storage in its manifest.
.Nm
takes the storage-granting role that
.Xr capsule 8
previously performed inline.
.Pp
A nonzero requested quota, or the configured
.Li default_refquota
when no override is requested, must be installed before writable storage is
delivered.
If that property update fails, the request fails with the storage error;
the daemon does not return an unbounded writable handle as a fallback.
Existing contents are retained.
A read-only view does not change quota or ownership and remains available
for inspection or recovery, subject to the ordinary access checks.
A zero configured default disables this per-claim quota when no override is
requested.
New claims, snapshots, and transaction staging clones are also subject to
object-count admission checks.
Failure to enumerate or decode the existing objects denies creation and returns
the underlying error; reaching the configured implementation limit returns
.Er EDQUOT .
Reopening an existing claim does not consume a new-object admission slot.
.Pp
Durable storage lives in a per-bundle
.Em container ,
.Pa Data/<bundle>/<unit>/persistent
and
.Pa Data/<bundle>/<unit>/cache
under the persistent root, with
.Pa Data/<bundle>/shared
for the bundle's units and
.Pa Data/Shared/<group>
for a group of bundles that declare it.
.Nm
reconciles those containers against the installed and running bundles
.Pq the install folders and Pa /Capabilities/Run
once at boot and then periodically, reporting containers whose owners are absent.
Catalogue absence does not authorize persistent-data deletion.
Bundle and group containers, including snapshots, are retained and reported with
.Er EBUSY
until an authenticated retention and deletion protocol can authorize removal.
This also preserves state potentially needed by retained boot environments.
Automatic persistent-container reclamation is therefore suspended; retained data
continues to consume storage and requires a future explicit lifecycle mechanism.
The bundle scan requires readable System and Run/live directories.
An absent optional Apps directory is allowed; other errors opening or reading
owner sources prevent reconciliation for that pass.
An incomplete pass resets the timer history.
Missing group-owner information likewise resets the group history.
The cadence of the timer passes, which is also the grace window, is the
.Va reclaim_interval
configuration key in seconds
.Pq default 300, accepted range 10 to 86400 ;
an out-of-range value is a configuration error.
The passes and refused reclamation attempts are visible through the
.Ql BSDFilesystem:::reclaim-pass ,
.Ql BSDFilesystem:::reclaim-destroy ,
and
.Ql BSDFilesystem:::reclaim-snapshot
DTrace probes.
.Pp
ZFS is a required subsystem for this system, although the host may still boot
from UFS.
.Nm
comes up in the PID 1 boot chain, after the pool is available, and serves
storage to consumers on demand.
On installer media, before a root pool exists, it remains available in a
degraded isolated-path-only mode so boot services can obtain policy and identity
files; dataset operations fail with
.Er ENXIO
until the configured pool is available.
.Pp
The maintained service unit starts in capability mode and receives explicit
configuration and device directory resources from SwitchBoard.
It acquires storage handles at startup and serves requests using retained
capabilities.
The acquisition path uses
.Pa /dev/zfs
and closes that descriptor after acquiring the bounded dataset handles.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl c Ar config"
.It Fl c Ar config
Read configuration from
.Ar config
instead of
.Pa /Capabilities/Config/bsdfilesystem.ucl .
An explicitly selected file must exist and have trusted ownership and mode.
Invalid supplied configuration is a fatal startup error.
An absent default configuration permits built-in defaults.
See
.Xr tzfs.conf 5 .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /var/run/BSDFilesystem.ready" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/Config/bsdfilesystem.ucl
configuration file
.It Pa /var/run/BSDFilesystem.ready
readiness marker, written once serving begins
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTOR DELIVERY
Descriptor-hardening failures are returned to the caller without attaching a
file descriptor.
The broker closes its local descriptor on either outcome.
Isolated-open ioctl grants use a whitelist selected by both client identity
and device path.
Unmatched grants receive an empty ioctl whitelist.
The virtual-HID whitelist applies only to the Blued client opening
.Pa /dev/vhid
or a decimal-numbered unit; the PF whitelist applies only to the Network client
opening
.Pa /dev/pf .
.Sh OPENENDPOINTSECURITY FACTORY
The shipped unit also exposes
.Va system.OES
with the endpoint requirement
.Va system.oes .
Requesting programs declare
.Li attributes = ["system.oes"]
in their unit UCL; SwitchBoard applies the software-attribute admission policy.
Clients use
.Fn oes_client_create_brokered
from
.Xr liboes 3 .
.Pp
The broker speaks
.Li bsd.oes.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/oes_broker.fidl :
each factory session answers
.Sy GetProtocolInfo
and then one
.Sy OpenNotify ,
whose reply carries the descriptor, and expires after five seconds.
At most 64 factory sessions may be active, including at most four per client
label; ordinary filesystem workers do not count toward these factory limits.
These limits count sessions, not delivered descriptors.
The kernel OES client limit bounds outstanding clients globally.
.Pp
The descriptor permits NOTIFY subscriptions and read-only inspection only.
AUTH subscriptions, writes, mode changes, scope changes, and further descriptor
transfer are denied.
The client has global event scope; its original opener identity belongs to the
broker worker, not the recipient.
Recipient-scoped descendant monitoring is not provided by this factory.
The descriptor remains usable after the factory worker exits and closes on its
last reference; factory-session closure does not revoke delivered descriptors.
Ordinary filesystem opens refuse the OES device, including normalized path
aliases and character-device aliases detected after opening.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Startup preserves reserved transaction datasets and reports unresolved work.
A reserved name is insufficient evidence that the dataset is disposable:
an interrupted commit can leave the original claim under a staging name.
Automatic resolution and reclamation require a durable recovery protocol.
Idle sweeps also preserve unresolved staging datasets.
Automatic staging reclamation is suspended until owner lifetime and durable
publication can be established; abandoned staging can therefore retain space.
.Pp
Commit refuses a failed staging-marker transition before dropping either mount
anchor, promoting a clone or renaming a dataset.
This prevents that error path from exposing a live staging clone to idle cleanup.
.Pp
Abort verifies the staging clone's recorded origin before releasing mount anchors
or deleting storage. Missing, unreadable, empty, or mismatched metadata refuses
the operation; an interrupted commit with a cleared marker requires recovery.
This check does not serialize concurrent workers or prove dataset identity across
rename/replacement races. Durable identities and coordinated lifecycle ownership
remain required.
.Pp
Transaction commit promotes a clone and performs two separate dataset renames.
There is no durable commit record or recovery protocol for interruptions between
these operations; error rollback is best-effort.
Commit is not an atomic namespace replacement and is not a system-generation
activation mechanism.
.Pp
Startup lease and transaction-staging sweeps do not yet distinguish a new boot
from a provider restart during the same boot.
Surviving workers and delivered directory descriptors can retain old storage.
A failed dataset destruction does not guarantee that earlier child or snapshot
removals in the recursive sweep were rolled back.
These sweeps require further lifetime coordination before restart safety can
be guaranteed.

.Ss Client admission limits
Graceful quiesce is refused while admission workers remain.
On provider exit, admission workers are killed and reaped before a successful
shutdown acknowledgement; teardown failure prevents that acknowledgement.
At most 64 concurrent workers may serve one authenticated client label.
This count includes ordinary Filesystem and OES factory workers together.
The stricter OES factory limits of four per client and 64 globally also apply.
An excess connection is closed before allocating session resources.
Capacity returns when the provider removes the closed session or exited worker.
Existing sessions are preserved; clients must handle connection failure.
.Sh REQUEST ATTRIBUTION
Requests require kernel-supplied sender credentials.
When managed-program attribution is available, the service records it for the
current request; missing attribution does not by itself reject every request.
Capability-channel admission, namespace isolation, ownership checks and
operation-specific storage authorization remain independent requirements.
.Sh SEE ALSO
